<quiz>
<question type="stack">
  <name><text>Trapezoidal rule for a curve</text></name>
  <questiontext format="html">
    <text><![CDATA[<p>Use the trapezoidal rule with \(4\) strips to estimate the area bounded by the curve \(y = {@fx@}\), the \(x\)-axis and the lines \(x = {@a@}\) and \(x = {@b@}\). Give exact values.</p><p>(a) What is the strip width \(h\)?</p><div>\(h =\) [[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]] [[feedback:prt1]]</div><p>(b) Find the ordinates \(y_0\) to \(y_4\).</p><table border="1" style="border-collapse:collapse;text-align:center;"><tr><th style="padding:4px 10px;">\(y_0\)</th><th style="padding:4px 10px;">\(y_1\)</th><th style="padding:4px 10px;">\(y_2\)</th><th style="padding:4px 10px;">\(y_3\)</th><th style="padding:4px 10px;">\(y_4\)</th></tr><tr><td style="padding:4px 10px;">[[input:o0]]</td><td style="padding:4px 10px;">[[input:o1]]</td><td style="padding:4px 10px;">[[input:o2]]</td><td style="padding:4px 10px;">[[input:o3]]</td><td style="padding:4px 10px;">[[input:o4]]</td></tr></table><div>[[validation:o0]] [[validation:o1]] [[validation:o2]] [[validation:o3]] [[validation:o4]] [[feedback:prt2]]</div><p>(c) Estimate the area with the trapezoidal rule.</p><div>[[input:ans7]] [[validation:ans7]] [[feedback:prt3]]</div>]]></text>
  </questiontext>
  <generalfeedback format="html">
    <text><![CDATA[<p>(a) \(h = \dfrac{b - a}{n} = \dfrac{ {@b@} - {@a@} }{4} = {@exd(hh)@}\).</p><p>(b) The ordinates are the heights at \(x = {@a@}, {@exd(a+hh)@}, \ldots, {@b@}\):</p><table border="1" style="border-collapse:collapse;text-align:center;"><tr><th style="padding:4px 10px;">\(x\)</th>[[foreach u="xs"]]<td style="padding:4px 10px;">\({@exd(u)@}\)</td>[[/foreach]]</tr><tr><th style="padding:4px 10px;">\(y\)</th>[[foreach u="ys"]]<td style="padding:4px 10px;">\({@exd(u)@}\)</td>[[/foreach]]</tr></table><p>(c) Each inner ordinate is a side of two trapezia, so it is counted twice:</p><p>\[A \approx \tfrac{1}{2} \times {@exd(hh)@} \times \left[{@exd(ys[1])@} + {@exd(ys[5])@} + 2({@exd(ys[2])@} + {@exd(ys[3])@} + {@exd(ys[4])@})\right] = {@exd(Ta)@}\]</p><p>[[if test="up"]]The curve bends upwards on this interval, so the chords lie above it and this estimate is too big.[[else]]The curve bends downwards on this interval, so the chords lie below it and this estimate is too small.[[/if]]</p>]]></text>
  </generalfeedback>
  <defaultgrade>3</defaultgrade>
  <penalty>0.1</penalty>
  <hidden>0</hidden>
  <idnumber></idnumber>
  <stackversion><text>2026063000</text></stackversion>
  <questionvariables>
    <text><![CDATA[/* exd: shortest exact decimal display of a terminating value (up to 3 d.p.) */
exd(v) := if integerp(v) then v else if integerp(10*v) then dispdp(float(v),1) else if integerp(100*v) then dispdp(float(v),2) else dispdp(float(v),3);
/* Design: complete a table of ordinates, then apply the trapezoidal rule; polynomial curves. Entries [a, h, c0, c1, c2]. Bends upwards (c2 > 0): entries 1, 3, 4, 6, 7 (estimate too big); downwards: 2, 5, 8, 9, 10 (too small). Source: adapted from SMP AS/A2 Core 2, Chapter 9, Exercise C, Q4. */
L: [[0,1/2,2,3,1],[0,2,66,5,-1],[1,1/2,3,1,1],[2,3,10,2,1/4],[0,3,5,4,-1/4],[2,1/2,5,-2,1],[1,2,4,1,1/2],[0,1/2,3,5,-2],[3,3,60,0,-1/4],[1,1/2,2,3,-1/2]];
ix: 1+rand(10);
a: L[ix][1]; hh: L[ix][2]; c0: L[ix][3]; c1: L[ix][4]; c2: L[ix][5];
b: a+4*hh;
fx: c0+c1*x+c2*x^2;
f(t) := c0+c1*t+c2*t^2;
xs: makelist(a+j*hh,j,0,4);
ys: makelist(f(a+j*hh),j,0,4);
Ta: hh/2*(ys[1]+ys[5]+2*(ys[2]+ys[3]+ys[4]));
up: is(c2 > 0);
/* diagnostics */
hwrong: (b-a)/5;
ysw: makelist(f(a+j),j,0,4);
T2: 2*Ta;
Thalf: hh/2*apply("+",ys);
Th: hh*apply("+",ys);]]></text>
  </questionvariables>
